: [[" kernel", -0.028713425621390343], [" Linux", -3.5944674015045166]]}, {"t": " source", "lp": -0.2990432679653168, "top": [[" source", -0.2990432679653168], [" sources", -1.9143482446670532]]}, {"t": " (`", "lp": -0.19508184492588043, "top": [[" (`", -0.19508184492588043], ["\n", -2.3795130252838135]]}, {"t": "Documentation", "lp": -0.25348883867263794, "top": [["Documentation", -0.25348883867263794], ["drivers", -1.606313943862915]]}, {"t": "/g", "lp": -0.1734929233789444, "top": [["/g", -0.1734929233789444], ["/admin", -2.467583179473877]]}, {"t": "pu", "lp": -0.007292330730706453, "top": [["pu", -0.007292330730706453], ["fx", -5.03075647354126]]}, {"t": "/", "lp": -7.534310861956328e-05, "top": [["/", -7.534310861956328e-05], ["/x", -10.74541187286377]]}, {"t": "xe", "lp": -0.008072505705058575, "top": [["xe", -0.008072505705058575], ["adr", -6.241264820098877]]}, {"t": ".r", "lp": -0.002248488599434495, "top": [[".r", -0.002248488599434495], ["`)", -7.205785274505615]]}, {"t": "st", "lp": -0.0004629968316294253, "top": [["st", -0.0004629968316294253], ["sti", -9.183457374572754]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) contexts are handled by the Xe driver. Here's what each mode does:\n\n## CCS Mode 1\n- **Context stealing**: The driver will \"steal\" a CCS from another GPU context when you need one but all are in use\n- More flexible but can cause **contention** and performance issues if contexts are competing for CCS resources\n- Useful when you want the driver to be adaptive about CCS allocation\n\n## CCS Mode 2\n- **No stealing**: The driver will not steal CCS from other contexts\n- If no CCS is available, the operation may **fail** rather than steal\n- More **predictable** behavior\u2014better for workloads where you want explicit control over resource allocation\n- Generally the **safer default** for most workloads\n\n## CCS Mode 4\n- This is typically the mode where CCS allocation is **fully managed/automatic** by the driver\n- The driver handles CCS assignment transparently without explicit stealing semantics\n\n---\n\n## When to use which\n\n| Mode | Best for |\n|------|----------|\n| **1** | Maximum flexibility, benchmarking, when you need CCS but allocation is uncertain |\n| **2** | Production/stable workloads, predictable behavior, avoiding contention |\n| **4** | Automatic management, general use when you don't want to think about CCS |\n\n---\n\n**A note of caution:** I'm not entirely certain about the exact semantics of mode 4 versus the others, and these details can vary between driver versions. The Xe driver's CCS handling has evolved over time.\n\nFor the most accurate and current information, I'd recommend checking:\n- The **`xe` driver documentation** in your kernel source (`Documentation/gpu/xe.rst"}